# 169 · Agents in their own terminal: each agent program runs its TUI, the live view shows it, steering types into it, session logs become events

**Status:** `READY` · **Version:** [v0.1.1](../roadmap.md#v011) · **Depends on:** [166](166-agent-definitions.goal)

## What

Each agent runs in its program's own terminal UI (Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode or Pi), not in the program's headless mode.

1. **The TUI, with nothing to answer.** Each program runs in its most permissive mode, with every permission prompt, trust dialog, onboarding screen and other popup turned off, inside the same sandbox as today.
2. **The live view is the terminal.** Control's live view shows the agent's terminal as it is, as herdr does and as the Hermes agent's dashboard does.
3. **Steering types into it.** A message from the owner goes into the TUI's input and is submitted there, so the owner can also call the program's skills and slash commands and reconnect its MCP servers. Each input is recorded.
4. **Session logs become events.** The harness reads each program's own session log, not its screen or a headless stream, and records tool calls, messages, usage, turns and the end of each turn as events.
5. **Fewer tokens.** The owner reports that Claude Code's `-p` mode uses about 1.7 times the tokens of its TUI. Thermite measures both on its own runs and records the result here.

## Why

The owner, 2026-10-26: "I also want all agents that ran to use native TUI of the harness it is running in in yolo mode with waiting/prompt popups disabled etc so that our live view of the agents then can just display that terminal. Similar to how herdr does it or hermes dashboard when it shows terminal hermes on the web ui. THis means when we steer it goes directly into the TUI input and submission, allows us to call skills, reconnect MCPs, etc. We then need to attach on the back end to session logs to record those as events and latest actions etc vs watching the json stream or headless stream of the system. (This will reduce our token usage on claude code as well as its -p usage is 1.7x higher than its TUI usage."

## Constraints

- **Isolation holds.** The TUI runs as the lane's user, with the lane's devices and network rules; agents never reach the judge's state.
- **No default caps.** A trial ends by its configured limits and its own end, as today; the TUI adds none.
- **Every input is recorded:** who typed what, when, into which agent.
- **New dependencies and downloads** (a terminal library, a browser terminal) come with the owner's yes, and each is credited in [CREDITS.md](../../../CREDITS.md).
- **Docs follow the code,** in the same commit.

## Done when

- [ ] Each of the four programs runs its TUI in a trial, with no prompt or popup that waits, and the trial ends by its limits or its own end.
- [ ] The dashboard's live view shows the terminal, and steering from the dashboard, REST and MCP types into it.
- [ ] One adapter for each program turns its session log into events, with tests on recorded logs.
- [ ] The token use of Claude Code's TUI and `-p` mode is measured on the same work, and the result is recorded here.
- [ ] The docs describe the live view, steering and what each program records; the fast checks, the docs checks and the agents, Claude Code, real Claude Code and Codex acceptance checks pass.

## What the review found

The audit's lane 12 (`.internal/reviews/v0.1.1-audit/12-native-tui.md`) reviewed each program's terminal UI, its session log and the terminal plumbing on 2026-10-26, from the programs' documentation and source and from Thermite's code. It ran and installed nothing.

- **Each program can run its TUI with nothing to answer,** given the right flags and seeded settings: Claude Code in bypass-permissions mode with onboarding and the trust dialog marked done; Codex with its bypass flags and the workspace marked trusted (its yolo flag no longer skips the trust prompt); OpenCode as a server with its TUI attached. Each needs checking on the pinned versions.
- **Each program writes a session log** that can become Thermite's events, and has hooks or a plugin that can signal the end of a turn. The log formats are not promised stable, so each adapter counts records it does not know, and a check catches a new version's change. Claude Code's transcript is readable by its own user only, so the lane keeps a mirror.
- **Steering can arrive inside a turn,** through Claude Code's and Codex's hooks and OpenCode's session API. That works in headless mode too, and ends today's wait for the next turn.
- **The token claim:** the review found no source for a difference in tokens. An open report on Claude Code's tracker (github.com/anthropics/claude-code/issues/97074) measures `-p` using about 1.8 times as much of a subscription's 5-hour window per token as the TUI (1.3 to 3 times in comments), with the same tokens in both modes. So the gain is plan quota, on a Claude subscription only, and only if the TUI's own extra model calls (titles, a classifier, compaction when idle) are turned off. Thermite measures it itself (question 10).
- **What can go wrong:** a popup that blocks the TUI; typing that races the program's input box; a session-log format that changes with a release; a turn that ends with no signal, leaving a trial idle.

## Design (proposed by the review)

- **A terminal host for each trial,** a small Thermite program running as the lane's user inside the broker, under the lane's limits, its GPU fence and its network rules. It holds the program's TUI in a pseudo-terminal (`node-pty`) with a headless terminal emulator (`@xterm/headless`) that keeps the screen.
- **Decisions read the session log and the hooks, never the screen.** The screen is only shown.
- **The live view:** the terminal's frames, sent only for trials someone is watching and kept in memory; the transcript stays beside it. A byte stream waits for goal [168](168-message-bus.goal)'s decision.
- **Steering:** Thermite's own messages go in through the hooks or the program's API; typing is for the owner's console (text, slash commands, skills, MCP reconnects), each input a signed command, recorded with its outcome.
- **Which roles** (the owner, 2026-10-26): every role the owner's messages can reach runs in its program's TUI: the workers, the planner, the teachers, the auditor and the review panel's reviewers. The advisors run in the supervisor, so their terminals are held there. The roles that take no message (the model check, the teachers' merged-by model, the synthesizer, the final writer and compaction) stay headless, with a view-only live feed. Pi is studied for a minimal TUI (question 1).
- **The steps:** goal [166](166-agent-definitions.goal) and the table of agent programs first; a spike of each program's TUI in the harness image, with recorded session logs; the session-log adapters, beside today's stream readers; hook signals and steering inside a turn, in headless mode; the terminal host; the terminal mode in the one trial loop; the steering command; the live view; usage from the TUI; stand-ins, acceptance checks and a version check; the docs and credits. About 55 to 65 files, a third of them tests.

## Decisions

- 2026-10-26: the audit's lane 12 reviewed the programs and the terminal plumbing, and proposed the design above.
- 2026-10-26, owner: input typed into an agent's terminal is signed, owner only: text, slash commands, skills and a fixed list of named keys, each a signed command recorded word for word with its outcome.
- 2026-10-26, owner, in comments on the review's questions:
  - **Which roles run in a TUI:** "workers, teachers, planner, any bit that can recieve a live message. Others should be view only live feeds." So the workers, the planner, the teachers, the auditor and the review panel's reviewers run in their program's TUI; the model check, the teachers' merged-by model, the synthesizer, the final writer and compaction stay headless, each with a view-only live feed.
  - **Headless mode** is "only ... an advanced rig option, hidden away under toggles, default is TUI where it can."
  - **Claude Code's entrypoint is never relabelled:** "we just use the TUI natively and send keys to it".
  - **Taken as recommended:** `node-pty` built in the image with `@xterm/headless` and its serialize addon; xterm.js in the browser, kept in the repository, read-only at the agent's size; no terminal recordings (the session log and the events are the record); the token claim measured offline first, then in at least three short paired runs with the owner's yes for the plan quota; a kit may carry agent skills, reviewed with the kit, copied into each trial's home.

## Open questions

1. **A minimal TUI for Pi.** The owner: "the idea of the TUI is to allow owner steering direclty into the TUI and optimize experience. Investigate further on minimal pi TUI to show." To study: the `pi` command's own interactive mode, or a small terminal view around Pi's in-process session, and what each changes in Pi's isolation and tools.
2. **A full Claude usage window during a TUI trial.** The owner: "herdr can react to this, think more on how we approach it." To study: how herdr sees a program blocked or waiting and acts on it, and what Thermite records and does (a provider wait, a resume, a switch of model).

## Progress

- 2026-10-26 21:03 UTC: Goal written from the owner's words. Lane 12 of the audit reviews the programs and the terminal plumbing.
- 2026-10-26 23:34 UTC: The design and the owner's decisions are in; the goal is READY. Building waits for the owner's word: the owner set this phase to documentation and planning only.

